
/* AOG GRACE COMPASS — crosswalk source of truth (auto-generated from the 5 SECULAR crosswalk DOCX). 81 rows. dom: A=Emotional Regulation, B=Self-Compassion/Identity, C=Social & Repair, ALL=integration. risk: 0/1(★)/2(★★). */
window.AOG_CROSSWALK=[
{b:'K-2',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Identity',lesson:'U1·L1 — Who Am I?',scene:'Ch. 1 · Who Am I? First circle on the carpet. Each child names one thing. Maya does not speak; Ms. Calloway says: “Sammy already knows you are here. That is enough for today.”',chart:'Identity Web (K-2) — five branches: things I love / good at / care about / special / private. Sammy icon at center.',neuro:'Concrete rule: “You can pass. You can wave. You can stim. Being in the room is the answer to ‘who I am’ today.” Pre-teach the question; let the child draw the answer in advance.'},
{b:'K-2',dom:'A',risk:0,theme:'Identity · Emotional Literacy',lesson:'U1·L2 — Emotions Are Information',scene:'Ch. 2 · My Feelings Are Friends Sofia’s stomach hurts before reading. Sammy: “Feelings are little messengers. If you yell at them they get louder. If you listen they tell you what they came to say.”',chart:'Four-Face Feelings Chart (K-2) — happy / sad / scared / mad with simple icons. Posted permanently.',neuro:'Replace abstract feeling-words with body locations: “stomach tight = the worry feeling.” Use a feelings-thermometer card on the desk. Never insist a child name an internal feeling without a body anchor.'},
{b:'K-2',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Identity · Mistake-Tolerance',lesson:'U1·L3 — Everyone Makes Mistakes',scene:'Ch. 3 · Everyone Makes Mistakes The class learns that mistakes are part of being a person — adults make them, teachers make them, Sammy makes them.',chart:'EVERYBODY OOPSES (K-2) — drawings of grown-ups, kids, even Sammy, all with little oops-bubbles.',neuro:'Desk-card rule: “A mistake = a thing I did. A mistake is NOT who I am. Same as: spilling juice does not mean I am juice.” The literal logic lands.'},
{b:'K-2',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · Inner Voice',lesson:'U1·L4 — Inner Critic vs. Inner Coach (K-2)',scene:'Ch. 4 · My Inside Voice — Kind or Unkind? Sammy introduces the Kind Coach and the Grumpy Gus — the two voices that live inside.',chart:'KIND COACH vs. GRUMPY GUS (K-2) — two cartoon faces. Kind Coach: “You can try again.” Grumpy Gus: “You always mess up.”',neuro:'Externalize the two voices as concrete characters. Practice the Kind Coach script out loud, in unison. Do not require the child to detect which voice is internal — provide the script; they recite it.'},
{b:'K-2',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · Guilt vs. Shame',lesson:'U1·L5 — Guilt vs. Shame (Oops vs. I Am Bad)',scene:'Ch. 5 · Oops vs. I Am Bad Kenji (another classroom) says “I am a TERRIBLE person.” Marcus writes the first card on the Mistake Wall: “I knocked over a friend’s block tower. I said sorry. We rebuilt it. I walk slower past blocks now.”',chart:'OOPS vs. I AM BAD (K-2) — two cards: OOPS = what I DID (fixable). I AM BAD = who I AM (false). The Mistake Wall launches here.',neuro:'Sentence frame on a desk card: “I did ___. That is the OOPS. I am not bad. I am the same kid.” Three blanks, same order. Practice with low-stakes examples first.'},
{b:'K-2',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Identity · Growth',lesson:'U1·L6 — I Am More Than My Worst Moment (K-2)',scene:'Ch. 6 · I Can Change and Grow The classroom plants seeds and watches them sprout. Sammy: “You are growing the same way. A little bit every day. You cannot see it. But it is happening.”',chart:'I AM GROWING (K-2) — a seed → sprout → plant diagram with the child’s name beside it.',neuro:'Use a literal growth chart on the wall; mark the child’s height monthly. “You are growing in your body. The same is true for your inside.” The literal visual anchors the abstract.'},
{b:'K-2',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · The Heavy Backpack',lesson:'U2·L2 — The Weight We Carry (K-2)',scene:'Ch. 9 · Carrying Too Much Ms. Calloway puts on the blue backpack with the torn strap and drops stone after stone in until she is hunched over. “Some stones go in for a little while. But every stone has to come out eventually.” Each child gets a small gray-blue stone for their desk.',chart:'MY BACKPACK (K-2) — a drawn backpack with stones labeled worry, sorry, sad.',neuro:'USE THE LITERAL BACKPACK. Let the child hold the actual stones. Touch = comprehension. Build a sorting tray: KEEP / PUT DOWN / NOT MINE. The deepest concrete translation in the K-2 year.'},
{b:'K-2',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · Accountability',lesson:'U2·L3 — Real Sorry (Three Parts)',scene:'Ch. 10 · Saying Sorry and Meaning It Marcus role-plays a real apology with Sammy. The three parts: name what I did, say I am sorry, make it right.',chart:'REAL SORRY HAS THREE PARTS (K-2) — a finger pointing (“I name it”) + a heart (“I am sorry”) + a hand fixing (“I make it right”).',neuro:'Laminated three-step apology card in the desk; the child reads the steps in order. Do not require eye contact during apology — verbal completion of three steps IS the apology.'},
{b:'K-2',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · Friend Test',lesson:'U2·L4 — Kind Coach Practice',scene:'Ch. 11 · Being Kind to Myself The class practices saying to themselves the kind things they would say to a friend. Sammy: “Would you say that to a friend? If not, do not say it to yourself.”',chart:'TALK TO YOURSELF LIKE A FRIEND (K-2) — a child and their mirror image, both with kind speech bubbles.',neuro:'Pre-written kind-talk cards: “It is okay to mess up.” “You are still good.” “You can try again.” The child reads aloud in a quiet corner. Scripted self-talk beats improvised every time.'},
{b:'K-2',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · Release',lesson:'U2·L5 — The Letting-Go Practice',scene:'Ch. 12 · Letting Go — The Practice Each child takes the stone from their desk and, at day’s end, places it in a basket at the door. Ms. Calloway: “Put down the stone of one thing you are ready to stop carrying.”',chart:'PUTTING DOWN STONES (K-2) — a hand opening over a basket, releasing a stone. “It is mine to put down.”',neuro:'The literal action IS the lesson: hold the stone, walk to the basket, open the hand, hear the click. This sequence is the entire teaching. Do not require the child to verbalize what they release.'},
{b:'K-2',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · The Hardest Forgiving',lesson:'U2·L6 — When Self-Forgiveness Is Hard',scene:'Ch. 13 · When It’s Hard to Forgive Yourself Marcus brings up something he has told no one — a thing he said to Aaliyah, his three-year-old sister. He cannot let go of the stone.',chart:'SOME STONES ARE HEAVY (K-2) — a child sitting next to a stone, Sammy beside them. “You do not put it down today. You sit with it. Tomorrow you try again.”',neuro:'Honor a NO. If the child is not ready, do not push. Provide a “holding box” with a lid: “It is still here. You are not carrying it on your shoulders anymore.” Containment as compassion.'},
{b:'K-2',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Forgiveness · Validating Hurt',lesson:'U3·L1 — Your Hurt Is Real',scene:'Ch. 15 · Your Hurt Is Real Mia, whose feelings were hurt by something her cousin said, learns that hurts are real even when adults say “it was not a big deal.”',chart:'YOUR HURT IS REAL (K-2) — a small bandage on a heart. “Bruises you can see and bruises you cannot see — both are real.”',neuro:'Validate even when the hurt seems disproportionate. “You are not too sensitive. You are sensitive. That is your nervous system. The hurt is real.” Never minimize sensory-driven hurts.'},
{b:'K-2',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Forgiveness · vs. Reconciliation',lesson:'U3·L3 — Forgiveness vs. Reconciliation (K-2)',scene:'Ch. 17 · Forgiving Is Not the Same as Trusting Sammy explains: you can forgive someone in your heart and still not be best friends right away. Forgiving is for you. Trusting is between two people.',chart:'FORGIVING vs. BEING FRIENDS AGAIN (K-2) — two separate circles. One: “I am not carrying it anymore.” The other: “We play together again.”',neuro:'CRITICAL for autistic students vulnerable to bullying repeat offenses. “Forgiving = I put down the stone. Trusting = the other person earns it back, slowly, by acting safe.” Trust is a pattern, not a decision.'},
{b:'K-2',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Forgiveness · Narrative Identity',lesson:'U3·L5 — Narrative Identity (K-2)',scene:'Ch. 19 · My Story Is Not the Whole Me Maya, whose story includes living with her grandmother instead of her mom, learns that her hard story is part of her — but not all of her.',chart:'I AM MORE THAN MY STORY (K-2) — a child inside a circle, a small story-bubble beside them, not above. “The story is part of me. It is not all of me.”',neuro:'For a child whose difference is centered in adult talk: “Autism is part of how you are. It is not all of you. You are also funny. You are also a builder. You are also kind.” Name the other parts concretely.'},
{b:'K-2',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Grace · Definition',lesson:'U4·L1 — What Is Grace? (K-2)',scene:'Ch. 22 · What Does Grace Look Like? Sammy: “Grace is when you give somebody something they did not earn. A second chance. A kind word when they were mean. A hug when they pushed.” The class makes a Grace List.',chart:'GRACE IS A GIFT (K-2) — a small gift box handed across, giver smiling. “Grace = giving good when good was not earned.”',neuro:'Concrete examples ONLY. Not “generosity of spirit.” Instead: “Grace is letting your sister play with your favorite truck even after she broke the other one.” Three observable examples beat one abstraction.'},
{b:'K-2',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Grace · Charitable Interpretation',lesson:'U4·L3 — The Pause (K-2)',scene:'Ch. 23 · Giving the Benefit of the Doubt Theo notices that when Jordan does not say hi back, it might not mean Jordan is mad. Maybe Jordan did not hear. Maybe Jordan had a bad morning.',chart:'MAYBE-MAYBE-MAYBE (K-2) — three thought-bubbles, each starting with “maybe…” “Before I decide my friend is mean, I think of three maybes.”',neuro:'HARD for students who pattern-match on literal cues. Protocol: “If a friend does X, pause and think of three reasons it might NOT be about me, before deciding it is. Hungry. Tired. Did not see me. Then I check.” Make it a script, not a vibe.'},
{b:'K-2',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Grace · with Limits',lesson:'U4·L5 — Difficult vs. Unsafe (K-2)',scene:'Ch. 25 · Grace With Hard People Kezia talks about a cousin who is unkind. Ms. Calloway: “Grace can be quiet. Grace can be a kind thought even when you stay far away.”',chart:'GRACE IS NOT BEING A DOORMAT (K-2) — a smiling kid with a clear boundary line around them. “I am kind. I am also not for stepping on.”',neuro:'Safety teaching for students at higher risk of being taken advantage of. “If someone is unsafe — hits, breaks things, makes me scared — I do NOT have to be close to give grace. I can give grace by walking away and not saying mean things back.” Distance is a valid form of grace.'},
{b:'K-2',dom:'ALL',risk:0,theme:'All Four · Integration',lesson:'U4·L7 — Community Charter & Year-End',scene:'Ch. 27 · Year-End Celebration The class looks at the Mistake Wall — full of Oopses-and-Repairs from a whole year. Ms. Calloway puts Sammy back in the wooden box. “Sammy will be here next year too. But we have what we needed.”',chart:'ROOM 12 LEARNED THIS YEAR (K-2) — one chart with all the year’s icons: Sammy, the backpack, the stones, the Mistake Wall, the kind coach, the gift of grace.',neuro:'Provide a take-home review card with the same icons and rules. “You can take Room 12 home with you in this card.” Predictable summary = memorable summary.'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Identity',lesson:'U1·L1 — Who Am I? (Identity Web)',scene:'Ch. 1 · Who Am I? The cohort reconvenes for fourth grade. Mr. Patel introduces the Identity Web — five branches, drawn with the older kids’ more careful handwriting. Branch 5 is still optional.',chart:'IDENTITY WEB (3-5) — name in center, five branches: Things I Love · Good At · Care About · My Special Thing · Something They Don’t Know (private).',neuro:'Pre-print the five branch labels so the student does not generate categories AND fill them simultaneously — two cognitive loads, not one. Branch 5 “private” is explicit permission to leave blank.'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · The Critic Named',lesson:'U1·L4 — Inner Critic vs. Inner Coach',scene:'Ch. 4 · My Inner Critic vs. My Inner Coach Priya fills the Critic column in three minutes and stalls completely on the Coach column. She flips the paper over and writes “DEAR PRIYA, if Hannah said these things, I would say —”',chart:'INNER CRITIC vs. INNER COACH (3-5) — two columns of paired statements (“You are bad at this” ↔ “You are still learning”). Five paired rows.',neuro:'Provide the Inner Coach scripts in advance — generating self-compassionate language on the fly is the wrong ask for many autistic brains. “Read these three Coach lines aloud when the Critic gets loud.” Internalization comes later, with repetition.'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'A',risk:0,theme:'Self-Management · Regulation',lesson:'Regulation & Repair — My Regulation Toolkit',scene:'Ch. 5 · The Toolkit Mr. Patel introduces the Regulation & Repair Toolkit — concrete strategies the children build into desk-card kits. Box breathing, 5-4-3-2-1 grounding, the Kind Sentence to Self.',chart:'MY REGULATION TOOLKIT (3-5) — four icons: breath (box 4-4-4-4) · senses (5-4-3-2-1) · body (push wall / squeeze hand) · words (one kind sentence).',neuro:'The toolkit must include a SENSORY tool, not just cognitive: weighted lap pad, fidget, noise-reducing earbuds, hood-up signal. Pre-rehearse the toolkit when calm — never the first time it is needed.'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · Guilt vs. Shame',lesson:'U1·L5 — Guilt vs. Shame',scene:'Ch. 6 · Guilt vs. Shame The cohort learns the distinction. Guilt = what I did. Shame = who I am. Guilt is functional; shame is corrosive. Marcus, beginning to remember the bike-and-Aaliyah incident more often, listens carefully.',chart:'GUILT vs. SHAME (3-5) — paired columns: “About what I did” / “About who I am” · “I made a bad choice” / “I am bad” · “Leads to repair” / “Leads to hiding.”',neuro:'Two desk cards. Yellow: “I made a mistake. That is GUILT. It tells me what to fix.” Red: “I AM BAD. That is SHAME. It is not true. I am the same kid. I made a mistake. That is all.” Color-coded literal sorting.'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Identity · Wholeness After Failure',lesson:'U1·L6 — I Am More Than My Worst Moment',scene:'Ch. 7 · I Am More Than My Worst Moment The class makes a list together — “I am the kid who broke the vase AND the kid who babysits her cousin every Saturday. AND, not BUT.”',chart:'I AM MORE THAN ___ (3-5) — sentence frame with three slots: “I am the kid who ___ AND the kid who ___ AND the kid who ___.” Practice in unison.',neuro:'Provide pre-generated identity statements the child can match-and-claim, with a word bank of accurate options. Recognition is easier than generation for many autistic students.'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · The Critic Caught Live',lesson:'U2·L1–L2 — The Internal Critic in Real Time',scene:'Ch. 10 · The Weight of Self-Criticism PRIYA’S BREAKDOWN. The Bengal-tiger / jaguar group-project scene. Priya cries in Ms. Calloway’s office: “I am too much. I keep being too much.” Ms. Calloway: “That voice — is that you, or is that the Inner Critic?” Priya catches the Critic in the act for the first time.',chart:'THE CRITIC GETS LOUD WHEN… (3-5) — three triggers: when I made a mistake · when I am tired · when I am scared. “The Critic uses three words: ALWAYS, NEVER, TOO MUCH.”',neuro:'The Critic-Catching Card: laminated, in the desk. Trigger words on one side (ALWAYS / NEVER / TOO MUCH / SHOULD HAVE). Hear one in your head → flip the card: “That is the Critic. Not me. I will name three things that are actually true.” Pattern-recognition over abstract introspection.'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · Accountability vs. Self-Punishment',lesson:'U2·L3 — Three-Part Accountability Map',scene:'Ch. 11 · Accountability Without Self-Punishment Isabella’s garden-gnome scenario is referenced. The cohort learns the formula: OWN IT + UNDERSTAND IMPACT + REPAIR = DONE. Self-punishment after repair = STUCK.',chart:'ACCOUNTABILITY vs. SELF-PUNISHMENT (3-5) — green box: Own it + Understand impact + Repair = DONE. Red box: Use mistake as evidence + Replay without repair = STUCK.',neuro:'The DONE marker is critical for students who loop on a mistake for weeks. Add a physical “DONE” stamp the child uses on the worksheet. Visual + tactile + verbal closure: “Once all three steps are done, say aloud: DONE. The script is the door.”'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · Friend Test',lesson:'U2·L5 — The Friend Test',scene:'Ch. 13 · Talking to Yourself Kindly Priya, weeks after the breakdown, writes a Self-Compassion Letter: “DEAR PRIYA. If you were Hannah and you said these things to me, I would say…” She illustrates Hannah on the left, herself on the right, the same kind words in both bubbles.',chart:'FRIEND TEST (3-5) — left: TO MY BEST FRIEND (kind, fair, honest, without punishment). Right: TO MYSELF (often harsh, sometimes cruel). “Would you say that to your best friend?”',neuro:'Use a literal mirror, or place a photo of the student where the friend would be. “You are looking at a kid who deserves what you give Hannah. That kid is you.” Concrete proxy beats abstract self-talk.'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · The Heavy Backpack (3-5)',lesson:'U2·L2 — The Weight We Carry (3-5)',scene:'Ch. 10 (rev.) · The Backpack Returns Mr. Patel brings out a heavier version of the same blue backpack with the torn strap. The cohort, older now, can carry more stones — but the bending-over still happens. Marcus cannot put the Aaliyah stone down yet.',chart:'THE BACKPACK (3-5) — stones labeled: things I did · things I said · things I am hiding · things others did to me. “All stones get heavier when carried in silence.”',neuro:'Provide a Stone Sorting Worksheet — three columns: MINE / NOT MINE / SHARED. Many students carry stones that belong to others without knowing they may hand them back. Sorting is justice work.'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · The Hardest Self-Forgiving',lesson:'U2·L6 — I Am More Than My Worst Moment (Application)',scene:'Ch. 15 · When Self-Forgiveness Feels Impossible Marcus finally speaks about Aaliyah — a Saturday morning when he was eight and she was five and he said something cruel because he was angry. He has carried the stone for two years and does not know how to put it down.',chart:'SOME STONES TAKE LONGER (3-5) — a stone with a timeline: held · examined · understood · repaired · released. “Still carrying it is not failure. It is the work in progress.”',neuro:'Slow Release Protocol: (1) Name the stone on paper. (2) Identify repair already done. (3) Identify repair remaining. (4) If repair is complete, the stone may be put down even if the feeling has not caught up. The PROCEDURE supersedes the FEELING when feelings lag the right behavior.'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Forgiveness · Validating Hurt',lesson:'U3·L1 — Your Hurt Is Real and It Matters',scene:'Ch. 17 · Your Hurt Is Real and It Matters Maya, who lives with her grandmother and not her mother, brings up — in writing, not aloud — what it has been like to be the kid whose mom is somewhere else. “Adults say I should not feel bad about it. I feel bad about it. Both can be true.”',chart:'HURT IS REAL EVEN IF… (3-5) — “…they did not mean it · it was long ago · others have it worse · the adults minimized it.” None of these invalidate the hurt.',neuro:'Many students have been told their distress is disproportionate when it is proportionate to their nervous system. Hurts from sensory invalidation, masking demands, and social exclusion ARE real hurts. Validate without qualification.'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Forgiveness · Repair Protocol',lesson:'U3·L2 — Repair Protocol',scene:'Ch. 19 · Repair That Actually Works The cohort learns the difference between performative apology and real repair. “I’m sorry IF I hurt you” vs. “I’m sorry FOR what I did. Here is what I will do differently.”',chart:'REPAIR PROTOCOL (3-5) — (1) I name what I did. (2) I name the impact on you. (3) I name what I will do differently. “IF” is not in this script. “BUT” is not in this script.',neuro:'Distinguish REPAIR apology (after a specific action that caused specific impact) from APPEASEMENT apology (chronic self-blame for existing). Only the first is taught here. Disrupt the second — many autistic students have learned to apologize for being themselves.'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Forgiveness · vs. Reconciliation',lesson:'U3·L3 — Forgiveness vs. Reconciliation',scene:'Ch. 20 · Forgiveness Is Not the Same as Trust The cohort sits with the distinction at the 3-5 level: forgiveness is INTERNAL; trust is EARNED. You can forgive and still set a limit. You can forgive and still not be best friends.',chart:'FORGIVENESS vs. RECONCILIATION (3-5) — two panels. Forgiveness: “What I do inside me.” Reconciliation: “What two people rebuild together.” Forgiveness requires nothing of the other; reconciliation requires BOTH.',neuro:'CRITICAL guardrail for students at elevated risk of repeated victimization: “Trust is a PATTERN over time, not a decision. Watch behavior. Consistent change over many weeks → trust may rebuild. No change → trust is not yours to give and not your fault to withhold.”'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Forgiveness · The Three Shapes',lesson:'U3·L4 — The Three Sides of Every Story',scene:'Ch. 23 · Stories of Forgiveness in Practice The cohort hears three real-world stories at age-appropriate distance: a man who forgave a drunk driver; a girl who forgave her best friend’s lie; a woman who never reconciled with the parent who hurt her — and was at peace anyway.',chart:'THREE SHAPES OF FORGIVENESS (3-5) — (1) Forgiveness + Reconciliation. (2) Forgiveness without Reconciliation. (3) Forgiveness from a safe distance. All three are valid.',neuro:'Pre-script which shape fits which situation. Changed + apologized + safe → Shape 1 possible. Changed but rebuilding is not your job → Shape 2. NOT changed and unsafe → Shape 3, no obligation to engage. Decision tree, not vibe.'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Grace · Definition',lesson:'U4·L1 — What Is Grace?',scene:'Ch. 25 · What Does Grace Look Like? Mr. Patel introduces grace not as a feeling but as a chosen action — generosity of spirit toward someone who did not earn it. The class begins the Grace Gap Field Assignment.',chart:'GRACE vs. DOORMAT (3-5) — GRACE: a choice · from strength · protects dignity · I decide what to give. DOORMAT: no choice · from fear · gives up dignity · others take.',neuro:'Critical safety distinction. Many students have been trained to mistake doormat-behavior for kindness. Drill the difference: “If you cannot say no, it is not grace. It is fear with a smile.”'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Grace · Mercy distinct',lesson:'U4·L2 — What Is Mercy?',scene:'Ch. 26 · What Does Mercy Look Like? Mercy is named as the close cousin of grace — but distinct. Mercy is NOT giving someone what they deserve when what they deserve would harm them. “Mercy is the choice to spare. Grace is the choice to give.”',chart:'GRACE vs. MERCY (3-5) — “GRACE GIVES what was not earned. MERCY WITHHOLDS what was deserved.” Two sides of the same coin of generosity.',neuro:'Concrete examples paired. GRACE: “I gave Maya half my snack even though she did not ask.” MERCY: “Devon broke my pencil. I could have told the teacher. I chose not to.” Distinguish by direction: grace adds, mercy withholds.'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Grace · with Limits',lesson:'U3·L7 — Limits (carried into U4)',scene:'Ch. 27 · Boundaries from Strength Ms. Calloway, in a guest visit, names the Limit. “A limit is what you will do — not what you demand of the other person. ‘If you yell at me, I will leave the room’ — that is a limit.”',chart:'A LIMIT IS (3-5) — “…a statement about what I will do, not a demand about what they must do. From self-respect, not retaliation. Spoken once, then enforced.”',neuro:'Pre-script three limits the student can rehearse before they are needed: “If someone yells, I will say ‘I am going to step away’ and leave.” “If someone touches my body without permission, I say ‘no’ and tell an adult.” “If a friend asks me to do something unsafe, I say ‘no thanks’ and walk away.” Limits in the body before limits in the mind.'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Grace · Practice',lesson:'U4·L4–L6 — Micro-Moment Menu / Grace Gap',scene:'Ch. 30 · Small Acts (The Grace Gap Assignment) The cohort takes the Grace Gap Field Assignment home. Each child commits to three small acts of grace over two weeks, keeps a journal, and debriefs without naming the recipients.',chart:'MICRO-MOMENT MENU (3-5) — twelve small acts in a grid: “Smile at the kid eating alone. Thank the cafeteria worker. Hold the door. Compliment without expecting return. Forgive a small thing silently…”',neuro:'Pre-print the twelve micro-moments. Let the student SELECT three to commit to — not generate their own. Three concrete behaviors with clear scripts beat one fuzzy “be more graceful.”'},
{b:'3-5',dom:'ALL',risk:0,theme:'All Four · Integration',lesson:'U4·L7 — Community Charter',scene:'Ch. 34 · Community Charter & Year-End The cohort writes its Community Charter — what Room 18 became. “We had a Mistake Wall. We had stones. We learned what real apology sounds like. We learned the Critic does not get the last word.”',chart:'ROOM 18 BUILT THIS (3-5) — one chart with all four pillars: IDENTITY · SELF-COMPASSION · FORGIVENESS · GRACE. Below each, the cohort writes what they learned in their own words.',neuro:'Provide a take-home Year-End Summary: same four pillars, same key vocabulary, same five anchor sentences. Predictable structure = retainable summary.'},
{b:'6-8',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Identity · Mask vs. Mirror',lesson:'U1·L1 — Mask vs. Mirror',scene:'Ch. 1 · The Mask & the Mirror Amara writes “adjacent” in her summer journal — the word for the modified version of herself she has been around Brielle and Cassidy. She has lost track of which Amara is real. The Mistake Wall moves up to the seventh-grade hallway. Mr. Patel returns.',chart:'MASK vs. MIRROR (6-8) — Left: the Mask (what I show; perform; edit; hide). Right: the Mirror (real opinions; actual struggles; real values). “The gap between these two is where the cost lives.”',neuro:'Add the For-Autistic-Students panel: “This room is a place where you can be how you ARE. You can stim. You can look away. You can be quiet. You don’t have to perform.” Make masking visible as a documented cost — not a virtue, not an expectation.'},
{b:'6-8',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Identity · The Specific Mask',lesson:'U1·L1 (expansion) — Identity Audit',scene:'Ch. 2 · The Funny Mask, Now in Middle School Jordan, the funny kid in Room 12 and Room 18, finds the funny Mask harder to take off as social stakes rise. The laugh becomes a survival reflex.',chart:'IDENTITY AUDIT (6-8) — two private columns. Left: who I am when performing. Right: who I am when not. Never collected. Never shared.',neuro:'For students who use humor or scripted performance as camouflage, name it: “Being funny IS one of your real selves. It is also one of your most costly performances. Both can be true. The exhaustion is information.” Permission to drop the bit in the classroom.'},
{b:'6-8',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Identity · The Digital Cost',lesson:'U1·L3 — The Digital Backpack',scene:'Ch. 3 · The Digital Backpack Kezia’s Instagram has 312 posts in a curated beige-and-cream feed. Her grandmother has early dementia; her parents are in a long divorce. “The curated Kezia and the actual Kezia are far enough apart that, at one a.m., Kezia has trouble locating which one is her.” Marcus is still carrying the June screenshot incident.',chart:'THE DIGITAL BACKPACK (6-8) — the phone is a second backpack carrying every curated version of yourself. Searchable. Permanent. Heavier than the physical one. “The internet is an archive, not a moment.”',neuro:'Many autistic students are MORE digitally vulnerable. Pre-script three rules: (1) one hour off the phone before bed, charging outside the bedroom. (2) the 24-hour rule before posting anything emotional. (3) one trusted adult sees the accounts. Not punishments — nervous-system protections.'},
{b:'6-8',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · Internal Critic (6-8)',lesson:'U2·L1 — The Internal Critic',scene:'Ch. 8 · The Voice in Your Head Mr. Patel reintroduces the Internal Critic — older now, more sophisticated. The Critic uses three tactics: catastrophizing, globalizing, personalizing. “It is loud, not true.”',chart:'INTERNAL CRITIC · THREE TACTICS (6-8) — Catastrophizing (one bad moment = forever). Globalizing (one mistake = always). Personalizing (it must be about me). Plus the Compassionate Witness: the honest, fair alternative.',neuro:'For students whose Critic has been amplified by years of corrective feedback, the Critic often speaks in the voice of past adults: “Some of your Critic is your own. Some is borrowed from people who tried to fix something that did not need fixing. You are allowed to question which is which.”'},
{b:'6-8',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · Comparison',lesson:'U2·L2 — Highlight Reel vs. Blooper Reel',scene:'Ch. 10 · The Comparison Trap The cohort encounters Highlight Reel vs. Blooper Reel — their own bloopers vs. everyone else’s highlights. The comparison is structurally rigged.',chart:'HIGHLIGHT vs. BLOOPER (6-8) — “The comparison is structurally rigged.” Their highlight reel vs. your blooper reel will never be a fair fight. Everyone has both. Most people show only one.',neuro:'Use a concrete metaphor: a movie’s trailer vs. its deleted scenes. “You are watching everyone else’s trailer and comparing it to your own deleted scenes. That is the wrong comparison.”'},
{b:'6-8',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · The Friend Test (6-8)',lesson:'U2·L5 — The Friend Test',scene:'Ch. 11 · The Friend Test The cohort returns to Priya’s old practice — write what you would say to your best friend, then say it to yourself. Older now, the practice carries more weight.',chart:'FRIEND TEST (6-8) — Left: to my best friend (kind, fair, honest, without punishment). Right: to myself (often harsh, sometimes cruel). “Would you say that to your best friend?”',neuro:'For students without an obvious best-friend reference: substitute a beloved pet, a younger sibling, a fictional character they love. The point is the kindness-direction. “What you would say to Hedwig” is a valid Friend Test.'},
{b:'6-8',dom:'B',risk:2,theme:'Self-Compassion · Narrative Authorship',lesson:'U2·L6 — Rewriting the Script · AFSP safe-messaging guidelines · Counselor co-facilitated',scene:'Ch. 12 · Rewriting the Script  ★★ HIGHEST RISK The cohort sits with the fact that, for some of them, the Critic has been writing the script of their life. They learn they can — slowly, with help — rewrite it. Suicidal ideation is statistically present in 6-8; the AFSP Safe Messaging protocol applies.',chart:'REWRITING THE SCRIPT (6-8) — the Critic has written drafts of your life since you were small. You are old enough to start editing. The first edit is noticing the draft is editable.',neuro:'Requires the Crisis Resources page (permanently posted). Autism is associated with elevated suicide risk; the adapted version names this directly. Pre-brief the counselor before this chapter. Provide a private, written pathway to disclosure.'},
{b:'6-8',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Forgiveness · Impact vs. Intent',lesson:'U3·L1 — Impact vs. Intent',scene:'Ch. 14 · Impact vs. Intent Amara learns the formula: “Impact is what is addressed in repair. Intent matters for understanding but does not reduce accountability.” Brielle’s comment landed regardless of how Brielle meant it.',chart:'IMPACT vs. INTENT (6-8) — “What was meant” on one side, “what landed” on the other. The repair conversation is always about IMPACT first. Intent is for understanding, not absolution.',neuro:'Many students have been told their impact does not matter because their intent was good. The reverse also applies: their impact often DOES matter, and they should not be perpetually forgiven for un-repaired harm just because they meant well. The rule cuts both ways. Justice is symmetric.'},
{b:'6-8',dom:'C',risk:1,theme:'Forgiveness · vs. Reconciliation',lesson:'U3·L3 — Forgiveness vs. Reconciliation',scene:'Ch. 15 · Forgiveness ≠ Trust  ★ HIGH RISK Amara calls her grandmother after Brielle’s comment reaches her. Her grandmother gives the four-part teaching: Brielle was wrong; you are more yourself this year; you decide what to do; you will need to put Brielle down — for you, not for her. “Forgiveness, but the kind that does not include trust.”',chart:'FORGIVENESS vs. RECONCILIATION (6-8) — two panels. Forgiveness: internal, mine to give, requires nothing of the other. Reconciliation: external, requires BOTH, changed behavior over time, and safety.',neuro:'★ PATTERN-TRACKING GUARDRAIL ★ “Charitable interpretation” without pattern-tracking can train students to override accurate threat detection. “If this is the third time the same person has done the same kind of hurt — you are not failing to be charitable. You are PATTERN-MATCHING. That is data, not bitterness.”'},
{b:'6-8',dom:'C',risk:2,theme:'Forgiveness · The Practice',lesson:'U3·L6 — The Agency of Release',scene:'Ch. 16 · The Letter of Release  ★★ HIGHEST RISK Mr. Patel introduces the Letter of Release. Four sections: what you did; what it cost me; what I am putting down; what I am keeping. The letter is never sent. “It is a private ritual you do for your own nervous system.”',chart:'LETTER OF RELEASE (6-8) — four sections. Never sent. Private. “Release is not the same as okay. Release is not reconciliation. Release is not what they earned. Release is what you stop paying.”',neuro:'★ PRIVATE-PATHWAY PROTOCOL ★ Pre-brief the counselor. Provide a written pathway. For students without a Letter audience, allow drawing instead of writing. Allow incomplete letters. Allow re-writing across months. The tool is the practice, not the artifact.'},
{b:'6-8',dom:'C',risk:1,theme:'Grace · Charitable Interpretation',lesson:'U4·L3 — The Pause',scene:'Ch. 18 · Charitable Interpretation The cohort learns to Pause before deciding what someone meant. “Maybe they didn’t see. Maybe they had a bad morning. Maybe it is not about me.” Three maybes before the verdict.',chart:'THE PAUSE (6-8) — before deciding what behavior means, list three alternative explanations that are not about you. Then decide if you have enough information. “Most of the time, you do not. Defer the verdict.”',neuro:'★ PATTERN-TRACKING GUARDRAIL ★ Charitable interpretation is for ONE-TIME or AMBIGUOUS situations. For REPEATED, ESCALATING harm it is not charitable to override accurate pattern-recognition. (1) First time → charitable interpretation. (2) Second → name the pattern privately. (3) Third → trust the pattern. Distance is wisdom.'},
{b:'6-8',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Grace · Practice',lesson:'U4·L4 — Micro-Moment Menu',scene:'Ch. 19 · Small Acts (The Grace Challenge) The cohort takes on a two-week Grace Challenge. Three small acts each. They keep journals and debrief without naming recipients.',chart:'MICRO-MOMENT MENU (6-8) — twelve concrete acts: “Sit with the new kid at lunch once. Defend an absent peer. Thank the lunchroom worker by name. Compliment without expecting return. Cover a small mistake for a classmate without telling.”',neuro:'Pre-print the menu. Let the student CHOOSE three. The cognitive load of generating grace-acts is higher than executing pre-scripted ones. Choice is the freedom; the script is the scaffold.'},
{b:'6-8',dom:'C',risk:1,theme:'Grace · with Limits',lesson:'U4·L5 + U3·L7 — Difficult vs. Unsafe / Limits',scene:'Ch. 20 · Grace With Hard People The cohort talks about grace toward people who have not earned it and probably never will — grace from a safe distance. “Grace is not the same as access. Grace can mean: I am not thinking unkind thoughts about you, even though I am also not in your life.”',chart:'DIFFICULT vs. UNSAFE (6-8) — a person can be DIFFICULT (annoying, immature, exhausting) and still receive grace. A person can be UNSAFE (abusive, dangerous, predatory) and grace looks like distance plus the cessation of resentment. Grace ≠ access.',neuro:'★ SAFETY-CRITICAL ★ Many students have been groomed to mistake unsafe behavior for merely difficult behavior. “UNSAFE means: hits, threatens, damages your stuff, breaks your trust repeatedly, makes you feel unsafe in your body. If any of those, grace = distance. You do not owe access to people who have shown you they are unsafe. EVER.”'},
{b:'6-8',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Grace · Across Time',lesson:'U4·L6 — Grace Gap (community application)',scene:'Ch. 21 · Mia Comes Home Mia, who moved to Pittsburgh in fourth grade, comes back for spring break. The cohort, now seventh-graders, holds her differently than they did at nine. Grace as the continuation of love across distance.',chart:'GRACE GAP (6-8) — the gap between what someone has done lately and what you still extend toward them. The gap is grace. “Friendship across distance is maintained by what you decide to keep extending.”',neuro:'For students whose friendships have struggled across distance and time: maintaining distance-friendships is a SKILL, not a personality trait. The cohort modeled it. The cohort taught it. You can learn it.'},
{b:'6-8',dom:'ALL',risk:0,theme:'All Four · Integration',lesson:'U4·L7 — Community Charter',scene:'Ch. 22 · The Last Day Mr. Patel, end of seventh grade. The cohort has built the framework and walks out of Room 36 carrying it. The Mistake Wall stays for next year. The work was real.',chart:'ROOM 36 LEARNED (6-8) — the four pillars in adolescent voice: I AM (not the Mask) · I CAN BE KIND TO MYSELF (catch the Critic) · I CAN FORGIVE (release, with limits) · I CAN GIVE GRACE (from strength, not from fear).',neuro:'Take-home review card. Same four pillars, same key vocabulary, same five anchor sentences. The card is the portable cohort.'},
{b:'9-10',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Identity · Continuity Under Transition',lesson:'U1·L1 (9-10) — Identity in a Scattered Container',scene:'Ch. 1 · The First Day of High School The cohort arrives at Lincoln Park High. The school is bigger, the halls louder, the schedule scatters them. The cohort that has been their daily container for eight years is now a fragmented thing they will only see in pieces.',chart:'WHO AM I WHEN THE COHORT IS NOT IN THE ROOM (9-10) — the self continues; the Mask is now optional in every room, every period. “Identity is what you bring INTO each room — not what each room makes of you.”',neuro:'High school transition is the highest-risk identity moment for autistic students: fewer predictable adults, more unpredictable peers, escalating masking demands. Build an Identity Anchor Card — “Things that stay true about me regardless of the room.” Five lines. Carried in the wallet. Re-read at every transition.'},
{b:'9-10',dom:'ALL',risk:0,theme:'All Four · The Container Travels',lesson:'Pacing Anchor — Wall as Permanent Infrastructure',scene:'Ch. 2 · Room 104 Ms. Calloway is the SEL coordinator now; her room is Room 104. The Mistake Wall moves with her — it will be in this building for four years. The cohort knows the Wall; the other 200 freshmen are about to learn it.',chart:'THE WALL (9-10) — public anchor at the entrance to Room 104. “Anyone may write a card. Cards are anonymous unless signed. Repair is always the second line. The Wall does not get messed with. The Wall keeps the building honest.”',neuro:'For students walking past the Wall daily, predictability is the gift. The cards may change; the rules do not. Print a wallet-card with the four rules: “anonymous-okay, repair-on-the-card, never-mocked, kept-by-everyone.”'},
{b:'9-10',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Self-Compassion · Solo Critic-Catching',lesson:'U2·L1 (9-10) — The Critic in New Rooms',scene:'Ch. 3 · The Inner Critic Goes to High School Priya (first-person) catches the Critic twice in one geometry class. The Critic is louder in the new room because the cohort is not there to catch her. “The catching, in this room, has to be entirely mine.”',chart:'CATCHING THE CRITIC SOLO (9-10) — “The cohort built the muscle. The high-school room is where the muscle works alone. The practice was for this.”',neuro:'Provide a wallet-sized Critic Catch Card: three trigger words (ALWAYS, NEVER, TOO MUCH) and three quick counters — (1) name the tactic, (2) state one fact, (3) breathe four counts. The card is the cohort-in-portable-form for moments the cohort is not present.'},
{b:'9-10',dom:'B',risk:1,theme:'Identity · Refusing the Mask',lesson:'U1·L1 (9-10) — Mask vs. Mirror',scene:'Ch. 4 · Amara at the Locker Amara, in a new building with kids from other middle schools, watches herself almost slip back into adjacent-Amara. She catches it and walks to her locker as full-Amara. She does not perform.',chart:'REFUSING THE MASK (9-10) — “The Mask becomes habitual under pressure. You can refuse it in the first three days, or spend four years removing it. Refuse early.”',neuro:'★ MASKING IS DOCUMENTED SURVIVAL BEHAVIOR ★ Masking pressure peaks at high-school entry. “You can stim. You can be quiet. You can opt out of small talk. The new rooms can learn. You do not owe new rooms a curated self.”'},
{b:'9-10',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Forgiveness · Empathy at Cost',lesson:'U3·L1 + Caregiver-Fatigue Strand',scene:'Ch. 8 · Theo Notices Theo, the noticing kid since first grade, starts noticing a freshman named Rachel being quietly excluded at lunch. He routes it to Ms. Wong; the intervention works. But Theo is noticing more rooms than one fourteen-year-old can sustainably hold.',chart:'NOTICING IS A GIFT — AND A LOAD (9-10) — “The kids who see what others miss are valuable. They are also at risk of carrying more than is sustainable. Noticing alone, for too long, breaks the noticer.”',neuro:'Many autistic students ARE the noticing kid — the same pattern-recognition that makes them autistic makes them see what others miss. “Your noticing is real and costly. You may route what you notice to a trusted adult and then put it down. You do not have to fix what you notice. The fixing belongs to the adults.”'},
{b:'9-10',dom:'C',risk:1,theme:'Self-Compassion · Carrying What Cannot Yet Be Put Down',lesson:'U2·L6 (9-10) — When Self-Forgiveness Is Hard',scene:'Ch. 9 · Kezia Carries Something An estranged relative writes Kezia’s grandmother. The letter sits on the counter. Kezia stops sleeping for four weeks, fails a biology quiz, lies to Ms. Calloway in an email. Ms. Calloway finds her in the hall: “What is going on.” Kezia tells her everything.',chart:'CARRYING ALONE IS HEAVIEST (9-10) — “You are nine years old, in some part of you, right now. The old version of you who carried this is awake in your body. That is normal. Both versions of you deserve compassion. Both need help.”',neuro:'★ FOR STUDENTS WITH SHUTDOWN HISTORY ★ When something old surfaces, the autistic nervous system often returns to its earliest survival state — shutdown, mutism, self-care regression. This is NOT failure; it is protection. “Your body has done this before. It knows how to wait this out. The wait does not have to be alone.”'},
{b:'9-10',dom:'A',risk:1,theme:'Self-Compassion · Honoring the Body',lesson:'Regulation & Repair — Body as Information',scene:'Ch. 11 · The Party I Did Not Go To Sofia, who has had a worried stomach since first grade, is invited to a freshman party. She does not go, and thinks for a week she made the wrong choice. Ms. Calloway: “Not going because your body told you it was not safe is not failure. That is your nervous system doing its job. Listen to it.”',chart:'THE BODY IS INFORMATION (9-10) — “Your stomach, heart rate, shoulders, sleep — not problems to override. Messengers. The override-the-body protocol of adolescence is wrong. The honor-the-body protocol is right.”',neuro:'★ HIGH SALIENCE ★ Autistic interoception is often atypical and reliable signals are easily overridden by social pressure. “Sensory overwhelm, racing heart, GI distress, sudden exhaustion are NOT social problems to push through. They are data. If your body says no to a party, the no is legitimate. You do not need to justify it — including to yourself.”'},
{b:'9-10',dom:'B',risk:1,theme:'Self-Compassion · The Caregiver Burns Out',lesson:'U2·L2 — The Weight We Carry (Caregiver Strand)',scene:'Ch. 13 · The Wall in February (Theo)  ★ KEY CHAPTER February. The Wall has a hundred cards. Theo stands in front of it at 7:42 a.m. for four minutes and writes a card: “I have been the noticing kid since first grade. I am, this year, more tired than I have ever been. I do not know how to stop noticing.” He signs it “— T.” Ms. Calloway sees it at 8:15. She knows.',chart:'THE NOTICING KID (9-10) — “The kid who watches the room is a gift. The kid who watches the room is also at risk. Watching alone, for too long, costs the watcher more than the watched.”',neuro:'★ AUTISTIC CAREGIVERS ★ The noticing IS the autistic pattern-recognition working overtime, so burnout risk is high. Three permissions: (1) route what you notice and stop tracking it. (2) take a noticing-break — a period, a week — off duty. (3) tell an adult: “I am full. Please redirect anyone who comes to me this week to someone else.” The role is not the identity.'},
{b:'9-10',dom:'B',risk:1,theme:'Self-Compassion · Identity After Mask Removal',lesson:'U1·L1 / U2·L6 — Identity After the Mask',scene:'Ch. 14 · The Old Story Jordan, the funny kid since first grade, sits with Ms. Wong and starts working on the script the Critic has been writing for him. Jordan: “I have been the funny one because being funny made me safe. I do not know who I am when I am not being funny.”',chart:'WHO AM I WITHOUT THE MASK (9-10) — a blank Identity Web. “You will not know immediately. You will not know in a week. You will know across years. The not-knowing is the beginning of the knowing.”',neuro:'★ MASKING AND IDENTITY DEVELOPMENT ★ For students who have masked their whole conscious life, removing the Mask is a slow excavation, not one decision. “You may not know who you are without masking. That is masking having been very effective. Start with one room. One identifiable preference. One stim you stop hiding. Build from there.”'},
{b:'9-10',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Forgiveness · The Letter Applied',lesson:'U3·L6 (9-10) — The Agency of Release',scene:'Ch. 17 · The Girl Who Was Mean to Priya in Fourth Grade Priya, now fourteen, runs into the girl who was mean to her in fourth grade — now in her chemistry class. The girl does not remember. Priya remembers everything. Priya writes a Letter of Release. She does not send it. The Critic gets quieter for a week.',chart:'THE LETTER IN PRACTICE (9-10) — four sections: what you did; what it cost me; what I am putting down; what I am keeping. “The fourth section is the hinge. Without ‘what I am keeping,’ the letter is loss. With it, the letter is gain.”',neuro:'Many autistic students carry decade-old harms because memory is detailed and forgiveness is conscious rather than spontaneous. This is an asset here: “Your memory held this for years because it was real. The same brain that remembered the harm can write the release.”'},
{b:'9-10',dom:'C',risk:1,theme:'Forgiveness · Grief Held in Community',lesson:'Grief Strand (cross-cutting U2 and U3)',scene:'Ch. 18 · The End of the Season Darius, whose dog Pepper has been with him since kindergarten, sits with Pepper through the final weeks. Pepper dies in late spring. The cohort holds him — Marcus brings food, Mia FaceTimes from Pittsburgh, Maya draws Pepper from memory.',chart:'GRIEF IN COMMUNITY (9-10) — “Grief is not a problem to be solved. Grief is a weight to be carried in company. The cohort that learned to carry stones together can carry grief together.”',neuro:'★ AUTISTIC GRIEF MAY LOOK DIFFERENT ★ It may show as routine disruption, sensory regression, special-interest focus, or apparent flatness — not necessarily tears or talk. None of these are inappropriate. “There is no right way to grieve. Your way is your way. The cohort will sit with whatever your way is.”'},
{b:'9-10',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Grace · Generational',lesson:'U4·L1 — Grace as Gift (Generational)',scene:'Ch. 19 · Grandfather’s Brush Amara’s grandmother gives her a paintbrush that belonged to Amara’s grandfather, who died before Amara was old enough to remember. “This is something he made. Now it is something you have. The line goes through you.”',chart:'GRACE ACROSS GENERATIONS (9-10) — “What was given to you that you did not earn. What you give to the next person that they will not earn. The line of grace is what makes a family, a cohort, a community.”',neuro:'For students whose family relationships have been complicated by miscommunication or sensory differences: “You can receive a gift from someone you never met. You can give a gift to someone who does not yet exist. The line does not require a perfect relationship. The line just requires the gift.”'},
{b:'9-10',dom:'ALL',risk:1,theme:'All Four · The Long Plant',lesson:'Year-Long Arc — The Sentences That Return',scene:'Ch. 20 · The Sentences Come Back Maya, quiet since first grade, draws on a quiet Saturday and realizes a sentence Ms. Calloway read in second grade has come back to her: “Sammy already knows you are here. That is enough for today.” Maya, fifteen, hears the sentence and decides to paint.',chart:'THE SENTENCES COME BACK (9-10) — “What was read to you at six does not always work at six. Sometimes it works at fifteen. Sometimes at forty. The reading was not wasted. The reading was the planting.”',neuro:'★ LONG TIMESCALES ★ Many autistic students absorb language in ways that surface later — sometimes years later. “You may not have shown you were learning. You were. The fact that you are using it now is the proof. The teacher who planted it is not in the room. The teacher is in the sentence.”'},
{b:'9-10',dom:'ALL',risk:0,theme:'All Four · Portable Integration',lesson:'U4·L7 — Community Charter (high school edition)',scene:'Ch. 21 · The Last Day Freshman year ends. The cohort, scattered all year, gathers in Room 104 on the last day. Ms. Calloway: “You have learned that the framework works even when the cohort isn’t in the room. That is the test of the framework. You passed.”',chart:'ROOM 104 LEARNED (9-10) — “The cohort is not the curriculum. The curriculum is the cohort’s gift to itself. We carry it without each other in every room. That is the whole point.”',neuro:'Portable take-home: the Year-End Card now layers every grade-band’s tools. Same card structure as Room 12, 18, 36, now 104. “Same architecture. New rooms.”'},
{b:'11-12',dom:'B',risk:1,theme:'Identity · The Threshold Year',lesson:'U1·L1 (11-12) — Identity at the Walk-Out',scene:'Ch. 1 · The First Day of the Last Year Ms. Calloway, on the first day of senior year: “This is the last year I will have all of you in one room. Next year you will be scattered. So this year — we will be honest. This year, we will not pretend.”',chart:'WHO AM I WHEN I LEAVE (11-12) — “What I am taking. What I am leaving. What I am still figuring out. Three columns. Lifelong document.”',neuro:'★ HIGHEST-RISK TRANSITION ★ Make the leaving-architecture explicit: “You will need to know what regulates you, what triggers shutdown, who your emergency contacts are, what your sensory accommodations are, what you say when a roommate asks why you stim. Write each down in advance. The improvised version of you in September is not the right person to make these decisions.”'},
{b:'11-12',dom:'ALL',risk:0,theme:'All Four · Integration via Inquiry',lesson:'Year-Long Anchor — The Five Questions',scene:'Ch. 2 · The Five Questions Ms. Calloway gives five questions to sit with all year: (1) Who am I when no one is watching? (2) Who am I kind to, and who am I not? (3) Who do I forgive — and who do I not? (4) What do I give freely? (5) What will I take with me when I leave?',chart:'THE FIVE QUESTIONS (11-12) — permanent classroom poster, returned to in every unit.',neuro:'Provide a Five Questions Journal — five tabbed sections, one per question. The structure carries the abstraction. Five concrete sections beat one fuzzy “reflect on yourself.”'},
{b:'11-12',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Identity · Grief as a Layer',lesson:'U1·L6 (11-12) — I Am More Than My Hardest Year',scene:'Ch. 5 · Pepper, in October Darius, six months after Pepper died at the end of Book 4, talks about who he has become since the grief. “I am someone whose dog died. I am also someone whose grief has not destroyed him. Both are true.”',chart:'GRIEF IS A LAYER, NOT A LOCATION (11-12) — “Grief becomes part of you. It does not replace the rest of you. You are someone who has loved someone who died. You are also still the person you were before, with one more layer.”',neuro:'For students whose grief processing takes longer and looks different: explicit permission. “Your grief may have been routine-disruption, special-interest focus, long flatness, sudden tears six months later. None of that is wrong. The grief is yours.”'},
{b:'11-12',dom:'C',risk:1,theme:'Forgiveness · The Long Release',lesson:'U3·L6 (11-12) — The Agency of Release at Adulthood',scene:'Ch. 9 · The Letter Kezia Wrote Kezia, now eighteen, finally writes the letter back to the relative who wrote to her grandmother three years ago. The letter takes two months to draft. It is one paragraph long. She sends it.',chart:'SOME RELEASES TAKE YEARS (11-12) — “What you were not ready for at fourteen, you may be ready for at eighteen. Or twenty-five. Or forty. The wait is not failure. The arrival is the right time.”',neuro:'★ HONORING LONG TIMESCALES ★ Many autistic students process complex relational decisions across years, not weeks. “Your willingness to take three years to write a one-paragraph letter is not procrastination. It is your processing speed for material this big. The letter is right because the wait was right.”'},
{b:'11-12',dom:'B',risk:0,theme:'Identity · Integrity Under Stakes',lesson:'U1·L5 / U3·L1 — Label vs. Integrity',scene:'Ch. 10 · The Lie Marcus Almost Told Marcus, in October, is asked by a college admissions officer about a leadership experience. He is tempted to embellish a story about the basketball league. He doesn’t. He tells the actual, smaller story. He gets in anyway.',chart:'INTEGRITY UNDER STAKES (11-12) — “Telling the smaller true story instead of the bigger fake one is, in some real way, the entire identity curriculum. The college essay is not the test. The college essay is the report card.”',neuro:'Many autistic students struggle with embellishment because literal processing makes it cognitively uncomfortable. Reframe as strength: “You may have been told you are too literal. In college essays, do not round up. Your literal is your asset.”'},
{b:'11-12',dom:'A',risk:1,theme:'Self-Compassion · The Edge of Self-Management',lesson:'Regulation & Repair — The Limit of the Toolkit',scene:'Ch. 11 · The Toolkit Has a Limit Sofia hits a moment in November where the toolkit does not work. Breathing does not help. Grounding does not help. She calls her therapist: “The tools are the on-ramp. When the tools are not enough, the next step is the therapist. The toolkit has a limit. That is what therapists are for.”',chart:'THE TOOLKIT HAS A LIMIT (11-12) — “Your toolkit is for the everyday hard. When something is bigger than the everyday hard — call a professional. The toolkit failing is not your failing.”',neuro:'★ ENTERING ADULT MENTAL HEALTH ★ “For most days the toolkit is enough. For the days it isn’t — a therapist who understands autism is the next step. Not all do. You are allowed to interview therapists. You are allowed to leave one. You are looking for someone who will not ask you to mask in the therapy room.”'},
{b:'11-12',dom:'B',risk:1,theme:'Self-Compassion · Rejection Is Not Evidence',lesson:'U2·L6 — Rewriting the Script / Real-World',scene:'Ch. 12 · The Letter (Priya’s College Rejection)  ★ KEY CHAPTER March. Priya’s top-choice school says no. Hannah, her best friend since fourth grade, is in the room. Hannah: “The Critic gets to have this for ONE day. Tomorrow we start the work of catching her on this one. We have been catching her for ten years. We are going to catch her on this one too.”',chart:'THE CRITIC AND THE REJECTION (11-12) — “The Critic will try to make every real-world rejection ‘evidence.’ The work is to refuse to let one rejection retroactively confirm everything the Critic has ever said.”',neuro:'For students whose Critic has used every prior rejection as confirmation of a master narrative: “You will not catch the Critic alone on this one. You will tell ONE person within 24 hours. That person is in your phone right now. Decide who it is BEFORE the decisions arrive.”'},
{b:'11-12',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Grace · Generational Release',lesson:'U4·L6 — Grace Gap (Family Application)',scene:'Ch. 13 · The Conversation with My Grandmother Amara has the first real adult-to-adult conversation with her grandmother. Tea at the kitchen table. “Grandma. I am going to leave. I am also going to come back.” Her grandmother: “I have raised you to leave well. The leaving well includes not worrying about me. Those are not in competition.”',chart:'GRACE FROM AN ELDER (11-12) — “The grace of being released by the person who raised you. ‘I want you to leave well, and that includes not worrying about me.’ This is not abandonment. This is sending.”',neuro:'For students whose attachment to family is sometimes more concentrated than neurotypical norms: “Leaving home does not mean losing the relationship. The relationship is portable. Sunday phone calls, holidays, the texts she sends — those continue.”'},
{b:'11-12',dom:'C',risk:1,theme:'Forgiveness · The Adult Conversation',lesson:'U3·L3 (11-12) — Forgiveness vs. Reconciliation',scene:'Ch. 15 · The Conversation with My Mother Maya, eighteen, asks her mother the question she has wanted to ask since she was six: “Why didn’t you raise me?” Her mother — sober now, in therapy — tells the truth. Maya does not forgive her in that moment. She also does not reject her: “Mom. I am going to think about what you just said. I will tell you when I have thought about it.”',chart:'FORGIVENESS DOES NOT REQUIRE A DEADLINE (11-12) — “You do not have to forgive in the moment, on the way home, or by next Christmas. The forgiveness is yours, on your timeline, with no obligation to perform it for the person who wronged you.”',neuro:'★ FAMILY HISTORIES INCLUDING ESTRANGEMENT, ADOPTION, FOSTER CARE ★ “You are allowed to process this for as long as it takes. You are allowed to write the answer in a letter rather than say it. You are allowed to never close this question. The forgiveness, if it comes, will be real, not performative. That is the only kind worth giving.”'},
{b:'11-12',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Forgiveness · Partial Release',lesson:'U3·L6 (11-12) — The Agency of Release (Real-World)',scene:'Ch. 17 · The Conversation with My Father Marcus has a conversation with his father he has put off for years. They go for a walk. Marcus speaks. His father listens — for the first time, just listens. He does not apologize or defend himself. Marcus walks home not having forgiven his father, but having put down half the stone. Half is more than he has ever put down.',chart:'PARTIAL RELEASE IS REAL RELEASE (11-12) — “You do not have to put down the whole stone to count as having done the work. Half is more than none. Any release at all is a release. The whole-stone framing is the Critic’s framing.”',neuro:'For students whose all-or-nothing processing has made forgiveness feel like a binary impossibility: “Partial release is the actual shape of most adult forgiveness work. You can release the daily replay while keeping the limit. You can release the resentment while keeping the wariness. The categories are separable.”'},
{b:'11-12',dom:'ALL',risk:1,theme:'All Four · The Cohort as Liturgy',lesson:'U4·L7 — Community Charter (Closing Ritual)',scene:'Ch. 20 · The Cohort Dinner  ★ KEY CHAPTER Late April. The cohort decides to have one dinner together — all ten plus Hannah plus Mia’s mother. They eat at Sofia’s house. Each person says one thing they are taking from the cohort and one thing they are leaving behind. The dinner runs four hours.',chart:'THE COHORT DINNER (11-12) — “What I am taking. What I am leaving. Said aloud, in front of the cohort. Once, in your life, this is the ritual.”',neuro:'Pre-script. Provide the two prompts in advance: “I am taking ___. I am leaving ___.” They can write answers in a journal a week ahead, read them aloud, have someone else read them, or pass and just listen.'},
{b:'11-12',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Grace · The Cohort Reproduces Itself',lesson:'U4·L4 — Micro-Moment Menu / Generational',scene:'Ch. 22 · Marcus Is Asked Marcus writes a letter to an incoming freshman: “There will be a person in your dorm or your class who is hurting. You will see it before they say anything. You will want to fix it. You probably cannot. But you can sit next to them. The sitting next to is most of it. I learned that from a teacher named Ms. Calloway, and a frog puppet named Sammy, and a wall full of cards.”',chart:'THE NEXT COHORT (11-12) — “What you learned was given to you. What you give is the next cohort. The architecture reproduces by being practiced, not by being preserved.”',neuro:'Many autistic students have spent their education being recipients of others’ kindness. Senior year is the threshold where they become givers. “You have received much. You have noticed much. You are about to be in rooms where you can build what was built for you.”'},
{b:'11-12',dom:'C',risk:0,theme:'Grace · Across Distance, Across Time',lesson:'U4·L6 — Grace Gap (Long-Distance)',scene:'Ch. 23 · Mia Returns Mia, who moved to Pittsburgh in fourth grade, returns for the cohort’s graduation week. The cohort holds her — not as the kid who left, but as the kid who came back. “You did not leave the cohort. The cohort is portable. The cohort never lost you.”',chart:'DISTANCE DOES NOT BREAK COHORT (11-12) — “The methods are: regular contact, intentional return, the willingness to be inconvenienced. The cohort does not maintain itself. The cohort is maintained.”',neuro:'For students whose long-distance maintenance has been hard because calls and small talk are exhausting: “Group-chat reactions. One-line texts. Voice memos instead of calls. Once-a-year visits. Letters by mail. There are many ways to maintain. You do not have to do it the neurotypical way. You have to do it. The form is yours.”'},
{b:'11-12',dom:'ALL',risk:0,theme:'All Four · The Final Charge',lesson:'Year-End Closing Liturgy',scene:'Ch. 25 · The Speech Ms. Calloway at graduation: “You are about to walk out. You have been building a room inside yourselves for twelve years. The room is finished. It is portable. Take it with you. You will need it. Not today. Not next week. In the third week of October of your freshman year of college, you will need it. The room will be there.”',chart:'THE ROOM INSIDE YOU IS PORTABLE (11-12) — “Whatever building you are next in — the room you built in twelve years comes with you. You arrive equipped. The equipment is invisible. It is also load-bearing.”',neuro:'Provide a final wallet card. Front: the four pillars. Back: the five anchor sentences (or, in Divine Mode, the five Scriptures). “You will not be alone in the dorm room in October. You will have this card. The card is twelve years of architecture. It still works.”'},
{b:'11-12',dom:'ALL',risk:0,theme:'All Four · The Lived Architecture',lesson:'Year-End — Architecture in the Wild',scene:'Ch. 26 · The Scattering Summer between high school and college. Sofia writing Mia twice a week. Marcus and Aaliyah’s August 14 ice cream. Amara learning her grandmother’s three recipes. Priya at the library. Darius and Hope. Jordan writing his book. Maya painting for an audience. Theo working at a bookshop, slow. Kezia in therapy twice a week. Mia in Pittsburgh, packing.',chart:'THE COHORT IN THE WILD (11-12) — “The architecture, once learned, does not require the classroom. It runs in the summer, the dorm, the workplace, the funeral, the wedding — and in the third week of October when you cannot remember why you came to this school.”',neuro:'Final autism-affirming statement: “You may not look like the other freshmen in your new building. That is okay. You will arrive with more architecture than most of them. The room inside you is the same shape as the room your cohort built. The card is in your wallet. You are not arriving empty.”'}
];
